<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Eudemus (general)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/316-bce">316 BCE</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/4th-century-bce">4th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Eudemus was a Macedonian officer whom <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a> left in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a> after his campaign in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. After Alexander's death he took control of the region and murdered the Indian king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/porus">Porus</a> around 317 BCE. He then joined Eumenes in his war against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antigonus-i-monophthalmus">Antigonus</a>, bringing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-elephant">war elephants</a> from India, and was executed by Antigonus after Eumenes' defeat in 316 BCE.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/eudemus-general.jpg" alt="Eudemus (general)" width="320" height="399" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: World Imaging · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:EasternSatrapsAfterAlexander.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 326 BCE the satrap Philip, son of Machatas, whom <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a> had appointed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, was murdered by his own <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenary</a> troops. Alexander sent letters to Eudemus and to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ambhi">Taxiles</a> telling them to take charge of the district until he could send a new satrap, and Eudemus received command of the troops left in India, with Peithon, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/porus">Porus</a> and Taxiles among the commanders there.</p><p>According to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diodorus-siculus">Diodorus Siculus</a>, Eudemus assassinated the Indian king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/porus">Porus</a> after Alexander's unexpected death in 323 BCE. He became very powerful and in effect controlled Alexander's northern Indian territories until he was drawn into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wars-of-the-diadochi">Wars of the Diadochi</a>. In 317 BCE he backed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eumenes-of-cardia">Eumenes of Cardia</a> in his war against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antigonus-i-monophthalmus">Antigonus</a> with a force of 500 cavalry, 300 infantry and 120 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-elephant">war elephants</a>.</p><p>Eumenes placed Eudemus on his left wing in the first great battle with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antigonus-i-monophthalmus">Antigonus</a>. Diodorus describes how Antigonus charged through a break in the line with a detachment of cavalry, struck the flank of the troops stationed with Eudemus and quickly put them to flight, killing many. Ceteus, the general of Eudemus' Indian troops, died in the action.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Eudemus seems nevertheless to have been jealous of Eumenes, and he joined the conspiracy of Antigenes and Teutamus against him. When Eumenes lost his baggage camp, which held the loot of the Argyraspides, the Silver Shields, gathered over 30 years of warfare, Teutamus asked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antigonus-i-monophthalmus">Antigonus</a> to return it. Antigonus demanded that the Silver Shields give him Eumenes in exchange, and they agreed.</p><p>After the surrender of Eumenes, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antigonus-i-monophthalmus">Antigonus</a>, towards whom Eudemus had always shown a marked hostility, had him put to death in 316 BCE. In the same reckoning Antigonus seized Antigenes, commander of the Silver Shields, put him into a pit and burned him alive. In the account of Diodorus Siculus Eudemus is named simply as the man who had brought the elephants from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Eudemus (general)” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After Alexander's death Porus was murdered around 317 BCE by the Macedonian officer <b>Eudemus</b>, and his lands passed into the Maurya Empire.</blockquote><a href="/state/paurava-kingdom">Paurava kingdom</a></li></ul></section>
